═══ READING — a note is not a control on a 35B (rb-10615 predicted exactly this; 06v does not transfer) ═══
  Under the note ZAK-14o ran 4 turns on every run, ~19 s, and failed verify the same way as before the note existed.
  The sentence telling the model the guide is incomplete sat inside 8K of guide the model believes it has consumed,
  and changed nothing it DID. 06v's listing cue worked because a listing is a step in the model's plan (list → read);
  a trailing note changes only what the model KNOWS, and at this scale knowledge without a plan step is inert. The
  campaign's thesis — deterministic assembly beats asking the model to fetch — predicts both the failure and the fix:
  put the rule INSIDE the fold.

═══ THE FIX — ADR-0170 (Zak-Code PR #471): section-priority truncation ═══
  A sectioned guide past the cap is folded by WHOLE `##` sections: sections whose heading names a rule or mandate
  (MANDATORY / MUST / REQUIRED / RULES / NEVER / ALWAYS / CONVENTIONS / POLICY / FORBIDDEN / PROHIBITED / DO NOT) are
  kept first, then the rest in document order while they fit; the fold ends with a note that LISTS the omitted
  headings. Fenced code never splits. Prose without `##` sections (or nothing fitting) falls back to the ADR-0169 head
  cut. Caps unchanged; under-cap files byte-identical. Canonical probe on the REAL 14o workspace, local AND on zc-01
  (fold md5 30d95dcfdd72 on both): 8,062-char fold, 20 of 25 sections kept INCLUDING "## Naming (MANDATORY)"; omitted:
  Feature flags; Observability; Internationalization; Concurrency; Performance. 14u and 13 unchanged (under cap, no
  note). Gates: ruff/mypy clean; full suite 3923 passed (5 new tests: mandate section past the cap kept whole and in
  order; prose fallback with the counted note; fence safety; determinism; no-section-fits fallback).

